var e=1,t=`alts-vc-01`,n=`alternatives`,r=`Venture Capital`,i=`Funding companies that will mostly fail, on the expectation that one or two returns the entire fund.`,a=`core`,o=[`alts-pe-01`],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`[[venture-capital]] funds early-stage companies — often pre-revenue, sometimes pre-product — in exchange for a minority equity stake.

The economics are unlike anything else in this domain, and they follow from one fact: **most investments will lose most of their money, and that is the expected outcome rather than a failure of selection.**

A typical fund holds 20 to 40 companies. A rough shape of outcomes:

- roughly half return little or nothing
- a further group return capital or a small multiple
- a small number return several times
- one or two, if the fund is successful, return a large multiple of the entire fund

So the fund's result is decided by its best one or two positions. Everything about how venture is practised — portfolio size, reserve policy, willingness to keep funding winners — follows from that.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`power-law`,body:`This distribution has a name worth knowing: returns follow a **power law** rather than anything resembling a [[normal-distribution]]. The best outcome in a portfolio is not somewhat better than the average — it is often larger than everything else combined.

The consequences are counterintuitive and worth stating explicitly.

**Avoiding losses is not the objective.** A venture manager who tightened selection to eliminate failures would also eliminate the extreme winners, because the characteristics that make a company capable of returning fifty times are the same ones that make it likely to fail. You cannot select for one without accepting the other.

**Missing a winner costs more than making a loss.** The worst venture error is passing on the company that returns the fund, not investing in one that goes to zero. A loss costs one position; a miss costs the fund's entire result.

**Averages describe almost nothing.** The mean outcome of a venture portfolio is dominated by one observation, so it says little about the typical investment. This is the [[skewness|skew]] and [[fat-tails|fat tail]] material made concrete — and note that here the fat tail is on the *upside*, which is why the strategy is tolerable at all.`},{type:`concept`,id:`return-driver`,body:`**The return driver is access to the small number of companies that become very large, and the ability to keep funding them.**

Two distinct capabilities sit behind that, and both are genuine.

**Selection and access.** Getting into the right companies at all. The best opportunities are competitive, and founders choose their investors, so reputation and network are economically valuable in a way they are not in public markets. This is why venture returns are unusually persistent by firm: an established firm sees better deals *because* it is established.

**Reserves and follow-on discipline.** Continuing to fund the winners through later rounds, and declining to keep funding the losers. A fund that deploys everything in first cheques and cannot participate in the winner's later rounds gets diluted precisely where the value is being created. Reserve policy is a large part of realised venture returns and is almost invisible from outside.`},{type:`table`,id:`environments`,caption:`What helps and what hurts`,headers:[`Environment`,`Effect`,`Why`],rows:[[`Technological change creating new markets`,`Helps most`,`Very large outcomes require very large new markets. No new markets, no power-law winners.`],[`Open IPO markets and active acquirers`,`Helps`,`The winners must eventually be sold or listed for paper gains to become cash.`],[`Abundant later-stage capital`,`Mixed`,`Helps portfolio companies fund growth; raises entry valuations and can fund weak companies past the point of usefulness.`],[`Rising rates`,`Hurts badly`,`Venture is the longest-duration asset there is, so it reprices hardest.`],[`Closed exit markets`,`Hurts badly`,`Holding periods extend far beyond fund life, and distributions stop.`],[`Capital withdrawal from the sector`,`Hurts`,`Portfolio companies dependent on further rounds fail for funding reasons rather than product ones.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-blows-up`,body:`**Missing the winners.** Not a spectacular failure but the most common one. A portfolio of reasonable companies with no extreme winner returns roughly capital after fees, which for a ten-year illiquid commitment is a poor outcome.

**Over-diversifying.** Beyond a point, more positions means smaller stakes in each, so even a large winner cannot move the fund. Venture requires enough concentration for one success to matter.

**Insufficient reserves.** Deploying too much in first cheques leaves nothing for follow-ons, so the fund is diluted through exactly the rounds where its best company is compounding.

**Funding losers out of reluctance.** The mirror error. Continuing to support companies that are not working consumes reserves that the winners needed.

**Extreme rate sensitivity.** Venture value sits almost entirely in distant cash flows. When discount rates rise, venture reprices more than any other private asset class — and because marks are infrequent, the repricing arrives late and all at once.

**Valuation staleness.** A company that last raised at a high valuation two years ago may be carried near that level long after the market has moved. Venture marks are the slowest and most optimistic in private markets, which means reported [[tvpi|TVPI]] can be substantially disconnected from realisable value for years.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`interim-marks`,body:`**Venture interim performance is close to uninformative, and this is structural rather than a criticism of any manager.** Early in a fund's life almost all reported value is [[rvpi|RVPI]] — unrealised, carried at the last financing round, which may be old.

Worse, the last round price is itself a poor guide: rounds are priced with preferences and protections attached, so a headline valuation can overstate what common equity is actually worth. A company that raised at a large valuation with a structured preference may be worth far less to earlier holders than the headline implies.

The practical upshot: judge a venture fund on [[dpi|DPI]] and on the identity of its winners, not on TVPI. And accept that meaningful judgement is not available until year seven or later.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask about the reserve policy explicitly.** What proportion of the fund is held back for follow-ons, how the decision is made, and whether they have declined to follow. A manager who follows every company equally is not exercising the judgement the strategy requires.

**Ask how they get access.** For early-stage especially, access is the constraint rather than analysis. What makes a competitive founder pick them? A vague answer here is a serious answer.

**Look at the concentration of value.** What share of the fund's value sits in its top two positions? High concentration is expected and correct; if value is spread evenly, the fund likely lacks a real winner.

**Discount the headline valuations of structured rounds.** Ask what the preference stack looks like above the fund's position. A high nominal valuation with a large preference above you is not what it appears.

**Assess [[vintage-year|vintage]] effects honestly.** A fund that deployed into a period of very high valuations faces a mathematical headwind no amount of selection fully overcomes.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Most investments are expected to fail. The fund's result is decided by its best one or two positions.`,`Returns follow a power law, so missing a winner costs more than making a loss — and averages describe almost nothing.`,`Access and reserve policy are the two real capabilities. Both are hard to see from outside and both determine realised returns.`,`Interim TVPI is close to uninformative: marks rest on old rounds whose headline prices may overstate what common equity is worth.`]}],u=[{id:`alts-vc-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`venture-capital`,`power-law`,`return-driver`],concept:`power-law`,stem:`Why would tightening selection to avoid failures likely reduce a venture fund's returns?`,choices:[`Because a larger number of positions always produces better returns`,`Because the characteristics that make extreme winners possible are the same ones that make failure likely`,`Because venture funds are contractually required to make a minimum number of investments`,`Because failures are needed to offset tax on the winners`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Position count is not the mechanism, and over-diversifying is itself a documented venture error.`,`Correct. Unproven markets, unproven technology and untested teams are what allow a fifty-times outcome and what make failure likely. Screening out one screens out the other.`,`No such requirement exists. Portfolio construction is the manager's choice.`,`Tax treatment is not the reason, and venture funds are typically structured so this is not the relevant consideration.`],explanation:`This is the hardest thing to accept about venture: a high failure rate is evidence the strategy is being executed as designed, not evidence of poor selection. A manager with very few failures may simply be investing in companies that could never have been very large.`},{id:`alts-vc-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`venture-capital`,`reserves`,`return-driver`],concept:`return-driver`,stem:`Why does reserve policy materially affect realised venture returns?`,choices:[`Because reserves earn interest while undeployed`,`Because a fund without reserves gets diluted in the later rounds of exactly the companies that are working`,`Because regulators require a minimum cash buffer`,`Because reserves reduce the fund's management fee base`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Interest on undeployed capital is trivial next to the dilution effect.`,`Correct. The winner raises several more rounds. A fund that cannot participate sees its ownership shrink through precisely the period when value is compounding fastest — so it captures a fraction of the outcome that made the fund.`,`There is no such regulatory requirement for venture funds.`,`Fee base mechanics vary but are not why reserves affect returns.`],explanation:`Reserve policy is one of the least visible and most consequential decisions in venture. Two funds with identical first cheques into identical companies can produce very different results based purely on whether they could keep funding the winner.`},{id:`alts-vc-01-q3`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`venture-capital`,`marks`,`misconception`],concept:`interim-marks`,stem:`"This venture fund reports a TVPI of 2.4x at year four, so it is performing very well." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — at year four nearly all of that is unrealised value carried at last-round prices, which may be stale and may overstate what common equity is worth`,`False — venture funds cannot compute TVPI`,`True — 2.4x at year four is unambiguously strong performance`,`False — because TVPI always overstates returns by a fixed amount`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Early TVPI is almost entirely RVPI, based on the most recent financing round. Those rounds carry preferences and protections, so the headline price can substantially exceed the value of the fund's own position.`,`TVPI is standard and computable for venture funds. The issue is what it means, not whether it exists.`,`It may turn out well, but at year four the figure is an opinion resting on other people's old pricing decisions.`,`There is no fixed overstatement. The gap varies enormously with round structure and staleness.`],explanation:`The specific trap is that a last-round valuation is not a valuation of your shares. A round priced with a large liquidation preference can leave common and earlier preferred holders worth far less than the headline figure implies.`},{id:`alts-vc-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`venture-capital`,`rates`,`duration`],concept:`how-it-blows-up`,stem:`Why does venture reprice more than any other private asset class when interest rates rise?`,choices:[`Because venture companies carry the most debt`,`Because almost all of a venture company's value sits in far-future cash flows, giving it the longest duration`,`Because venture funds use the most leverage at the fund level`,`Because venture marks are updated most frequently`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Venture companies typically carry little or no debt — less than any other private category.`,`Correct. An early-stage company's value is almost entirely expectations of cash flows many years out. Those carry the largest discounting exponent, so they fall hardest when the rate rises.`,`Venture funds generally use minimal fund-level leverage, less than buyout.`,`Venture marks are among the *least* frequently updated, which is why the repricing arrives late rather than not at all.`],explanation:`This is the discount-factor table at its most extreme. Value concentrated fifteen or twenty years out is exactly the position the table shows collapsing when the rate rises — and infrequent marking means the collapse is recognised in one step rather than gradually.`},{id:`alts-vc-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`venture-capital`,`concentration`,`diligence`],concept:`on-the-job`,stem:`In a venture fund at year eight, value is spread fairly evenly across 25 holdings with no position above 8% of fund value. How should you read that?`,choices:[`Well diversified, which reduces risk appropriately`,`Concerning — it suggests the fund has no genuine winner, and venture returns depend on having one`,`Evidence of strong selection, since no company has failed`,`Normal for year eight, since winners only emerge in year ten`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Even distribution sounds prudent and is the wrong shape for this strategy. Venture returns come from extreme outcomes, and an even spread means none occurred.`,`Correct. By year eight a successful venture fund normally has one or two positions dominating its value. An even spread indicates a portfolio of adequate companies, which after fees and a decade of illiquidity is a poor result.`,`Even distribution is not evidence of no failures — failures would show as near-zero positions. It indicates no extreme successes.`,`By year eight the winners in a successful fund are usually clearly identifiable. Waiting for year ten is optimistic.`],explanation:`This inverts the usual instinct about concentration. In most strategies an even spread is prudent; in venture it is the signature of a fund that has not produced the outcome the whole structure exists to capture.`},{id:`alts-vc-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`venture-capital`,`access`,`on-the-job`],concept:`return-driver`,stem:`Why is access considered a genuine and persistent source of venture returns, in a way it rarely is in public markets?`,choices:[`Because venture investors receive information other investors cannot see`,`Because founders choose their investors, so reputation determines which opportunities a firm is offered at all`,`Because early-stage valuations are set by regulation`,`Because venture firms can buy unlimited amounts of any company`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Information advantages exist in diligence but are not the durable mechanism, and they do not explain persistence across decades.`,`Correct. The best founders can choose among investors, so an established firm is shown opportunities that others never see. That is self-reinforcing, which is why venture returns are unusually persistent by firm.`,`Valuations are negotiated, not regulated.`,`Allocations in competitive rounds are limited precisely because demand exceeds supply — which is the access constraint restated.`],explanation:`In public markets anyone can buy the same share at the same price, so persistent access advantage is hard to sustain. In venture the opportunity set itself differs by firm, which makes brand economically valuable rather than merely reputational.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
